    {
      "id": 69,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "PostgreSQL, Kafka and the rest",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "prometheus"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 40,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 6
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "These panels show what **the application** measures about its dependencies: the\ncalls it makes, how long they take and how often they fail. That is deliberately\nnot the same as monitoring the services themselves.\n\n**PostgreSQL** — the API holds a connection pool but makes no queries on the\nsearch path (each change event carries its row), so there is nothing\napplication-side to show. Pool and server statistics would need a database\nexporter.\n\n**Kafka broker** — consumer lag, partition sizes and broker health come from the\nbroker, not from this application. `scripts/kafka-lag.ps1` reports lag from\nKafka's own tooling, and `go run ./cmd/loadgen -mode watch` prints it beside the\nindex counts during a benchmark.\n\nNeither exporter is installed here: an exporter is infrastructure monitoring,\nwhich is its own decision, and inventing metrics to fill a dashboard is worse\nthan an honest gap. See `docs/metrics.md`."
      },
      "transparent": false
    }
